        'synopsis' => 'A Victorian scientist travels millions of years into the future and discovers humanity has split into two terrible species.',
        'text' => "The Time Traveler was expounding a recondite matter to us. His gray eyes shone, and his usually pale face was flushed and animated. He told us that any real body must have extension in four directions, Length, Breadth, Thickness, and Duration. \"Time is only a fourth dimension of Space,\" he said. He had been at work upon this geometry for some time and had experimental verification.\n\nHe brought out a glittering metallic framework, scarcely larger than a small clock. He told us this was a model of a machine to travel through time. He pressed the lever, and the little machine swung round, became indistinct, and vanished. We were all stunned. Then he led us to his laboratory and showed us a larger version nearly complete. He said he meant to explore time.\n\nThe next Thursday he arrived late to dinner, dirty and haggard, with a cut on his chin and blood-stained socks. He was limping. He ate ravenously, then led us to the smoking room and told his story.\n\nHe said he had started that morning at ten o'clock. He pressed the lever and felt a nightmare sensation of falling. The laboratory grew hazy and went dark. Night followed day like the flapping of a black wing. He saw the sun hopping swiftly across the sky, and the moon spinning through her quarters. The landscape changed around him, trees growing and passing away like vapor, huge buildings rising and falling like dreams.\n\nHe stopped the machine and was thrown headlong. He found himself on a little lawn in a garden, surrounded by rhododendron bushes, in a hail storm. Before him stood a colossal white marble figure, like a winged sphinx, with sightless eyes that seemed to watch him. He was in the year Eight Hundred and Two Thousand Seven Hundred and One.\n\nFrail beautiful creatures came toward him, perhaps four feet tall, clad in purple tunics. They had large mild eyes and delicate features. They spoke in a sweet liquid tongue. One asked him if he had come from the sun in a thunderstorm, which showed him they were like children. They brought him flowers and led him into a vast building where they ate fruit. All their diet was fruit. He tried to learn their language, but they were indolent and easily tired.\n\nHe soon discovered their lack of interest. They would examine him like children and then wander away. He climbed a hill to view the world. The whole earth had become a garden. Great palaces dotted the greenery, some in ruins. There were no hedges, no signs of agriculture. He saw no small houses, and all the people were alike in costume and bearing. The sexes had become almost identical. He theorized that humanity had conquered Nature and now lived in perfect security, and that security had made them weak and childlike.\n\nThen he discovered the Time Machine was gone from where he had left it. He ran down the hill in a frenzy, but only the empty lawn remained. He raved through the night, beating the bushes, searching everywhere. The White Sphinx seemed to smile in mockery. He slept, and in the morning he examined the ground more calmly. He found the pedestal of the sphinx was hollow, and he was certain his machine was inside it. But when he tried to open it, the little people reacted with horror and disgust.\n\nHe explored further and found circular wells with bronze rims. Down them he heard a thudding sound like a great engine, and a current of air set down the shafts. He began to suspect an underground world.\n\nOne day he saved a little creature from drowning. Her name was Weena. She became his friend and followed him everywhere. She was fearless in daylight but dreaded the dark and shadows. He learned from her that fear still lived in the world.\n\nOne morning he saw white ape-like creatures running in the half-light. Later, exploring a ruin, he found a narrow gallery. A pair of luminous eyes watched him from the darkness. A white thing ran past him, and he followed it to one of the wells. He lit a match and saw a small white creature with large bright eyes clambering down the wall like a human spider.\n\nHe realized that Man had differentiated into two species. The graceful children of the Upper World were the Eloi. The bleached nocturnal things below were the Morlocks. He guessed that the Morlocks were the laborers who had been driven underground, and the Eloi were the aristocrats who had lived above. Now the old order was reversing. The Morlocks kept the Eloi like cattle. They came up at night to feed.\n\nHe resolved to descend. He left Weena at the well and climbed down into the darkness. He found a vast arched cavern with great machines and a table laid with a meal of meat. The Morlocks surrounded him, touching him with their lank fingers. He lit matches to drive them back and barely escaped up the shaft, collapsing in the sunlight. Weena kissed his hands and face.\n\nHe knew now he must defend himself. He decided to make weapons and find a refuge. He went toward the Palace of Green Porcelain, carrying Weena on his shoulder. At night, as they rested on a hill, he looked at the stars and realized the old constellations had gone. He thought of the Great Fear between the two species, and for the first time he understood what the meat he had seen must be.\n\nIn the morning they entered the Palace of Green Porcelain. It was a vast museum, fallen into ruin. He found fossils, minerals, and great corroded machines. In a sealed case he discovered a box of matches, perfectly good. He also found camphor, which burned brightly. He took an iron bar as a weapon. He found two dynamite cartridges, but they were dummies.\n\nThey left the palace and pushed through a wood as night fell. He lit a fire to keep the Morlocks back, but he fell asleep, and the fire went out. The Morlocks swarmed over him. He fought them off with his iron bar, killing several. Then he saw the forest was burning behind him, his fire having spread. The Morlocks fled in terror. He looked for Weena but she was gone. He spent the night among the blind creatures, searching for her, but found no trace.\n\nIn the morning he walked across smoking ashes toward the White Sphinx. He found the bronze doors open and the Time Machine inside, carefully oiled and cleaned. He stepped up to it, but the doors slid shut behind him, trapping him in the dark. The Morlocks came toward him. He scrambled into the saddle, fought them off, and pulled the lever. The clinging hands slipped from him, and he was traveling through time again.\n\nHe had pulled the levers forward instead of backward. He swept further into futurity at prodigious speed. The sun grew red and motionless. The moon vanished. The earth had stopped turning, one face toward the sun. He stopped on a desolate beach. The sky was inky black to the north and deep red to the south. The huge hull of the sun lay red and motionless on the horizon. He saw monstrous crab-like creatures crawling toward him. He fled again, traveling in great strides of a thousand years or more. The sun grew larger and duller. The crabs disappeared. A bitter cold came. Snow fell. The world grew dark as an eclipse began. He saw a black shape hopping in the red sea, and felt he was fainting.\n\nHe came back, stopping the machine gently. He saw Mrs. Watchett move backward across the laboratory. Then he was in his own workshop again. He came in to dinner, limping, and told us everything. He showed us two withered white flowers that Weena had put in his pocket.\n\nWe did not quite believe him. The Medical Man examined the flowers and found them strange. The Editor thought it a gaudy lie. But the machine was there in the laboratory, solid to the touch, with grass and moss on the lower parts and a bent rail.\n\nI came back the next day to see him again. He was busy, but said he would prove everything at lunch. I went to tell him I had to leave, and as I opened the laboratory door, I saw a ghostly figure in a whirling mass of black and brass for a moment. Then the Time Machine was gone. A pane of the skylight had blown in. The Time Traveler vanished three years ago and has never returned.\n\nI wonder if he will ever come back. Perhaps he swept into the past among the savage brutes of the Stone Age, or into the Jurassic times among the great reptiles. Or perhaps he went forward to a time when the riddles of our age are answered. But to me the future is black and blank. I have by me, for my comfort, two strange white flowers, now shriveled and brown, to witness that even when mind and strength had gone, gratitude and tenderness still lived on in the heart of man.",
