        'synopsis' => 'When Martians invade Earth with deadly Heat-Rays and poisonous black smoke, humanity faces annihilation from a ruthless intelligence far superior to our own.',
        'text' => "No one would have believed in the last years of the nineteenth century that this world was being watched keenly by intelligences greater than man's. Yet across the gulf of space, minds vast and cool regarded this earth with envious eyes, and slowly drew their plans against us. Early in the twentieth century came the great disillusionment.\n\nThe planet Mars is older than our earth and nearer its end. Its oceans have shrunk, its seasons bring huge snowcaps that inundate its temperate zones. The pressure of necessity has hardened the Martians' hearts and brightened their intellects. They saw our warmer planet as a morning star of hope.\n\nFor ten nights observers saw a flame each night from Mars. I watched with Ogilvy the astronomer at Ottershaw. He thought it might be volcanic activity. He told me, \"The chances against anything manlike on Mars are a million to one.\" We walked home through the sleeping villages, never dreaming of the missile speeding towards us.\n\nThe first cylinder fell on Horsell Common. Ogilvy found it at dawn, a huge artificial cylinder with a screw top. He ran to Woking with the news. By midday a crowd had gathered. The cylinder slowly unscrewed from within. When the lid fell, a grayish bulk rose out, glistening like wet leather, with large dark eyes and a quivering mouth. A tentacle gripped the edge. The crowd fled in terror. I saw one man fall into the pit and vanish.\n\nThe Martians raised a spinning mirror. The Deputation approached with a white flag. A flash of light leaped from the pit, and men burst into flame. The Heat-Ray swept across the common, setting trees and bushes alight. I ran blindly through the heather, weeping like a child. Nearly forty people lay charred around the pit that night.\n\nI reached home and told my wife. I tried to reassure her, saying the Martians could barely move in our gravity. I did not yet understand their machines.\n\nThe next day, Saturday, the troops surrounded the common. I walked towards the fighting, full of schoolboy dreams of battle. From my window that evening I saw the fighting-machines for the first time, monstrous tripods of glittering metal striding over the trees. The Heat-Ray swept across Maybury Hill. I grabbed my wife and our servant and fled. We drove to Leatherhead, where I left my wife with her cousins. I promised to return, but I felt a fever to see the battle through.\n\nReturning that night, a thunderstorm broke. A second cylinder fell. In the lightning I saw a Martian fighting-machine striding towards me, towering above the pine trees. It howled like a siren. My horse bolted, the cart overturned, and I crawled into a ditch. I saw it pass, tentacles swinging, green smoke spurting from its joints. I made my way home through the rain.\n\nFrom my study window I watched the destruction of Woking. A soldier found me and told me the Martians had wiped out the batteries. \"They wiped us out, simply wiped us out,\" he said, weeping like a little boy. He had hidden under a dead horse while the Heat-Ray swept the common. The Martians had built themselves up out of their pits like Titans of metal. Now London lay open before them.\n\nThe artilleryman and I left Maybury Hill together. We passed through Byfleet and saw the guns waiting. At Weybridge the Martians came striding across the river. I threw myself into the water as the Heat-Ray swept the bank. A shell struck one Martian in the face and destroyed it. The others advanced, and their Heat-Rays set the river boiling. I escaped by a miracle.\n\nI met the curate in the fields near Walton. He was a weak man, weeping and quoting scripture, asking why God had permitted this. \"What does it mean?\" he asked. \"What do these things mean?\" I tried to reason with him, but he was beyond reason. We wandered together through the ruined country. The Martians began using the Black Smoke, a poisonous vapor that killed everything it touched. They spread it in canisters from their fighting-machines.\n\nThe great panic of Monday morning overtook London. My brother, a medical student, was awakened by policemen hammering at doors. \"The Martians are coming!\" they shouted. He heard the news that London was in danger. The Martians had forced the Kingston and Richmond defenses. He made his way north through the streaming fugitives. On the road he saved two women from robbers and joined them. They pushed eastward towards the coast.\n\nAt Chelmsford their pony was seized for food. They continued on foot. At the coast they saw an amazing crowd of shipping, and the torpedo ram Thunder Child steaming towards the Martians. The ram struck and destroyed two Martians before being destroyed itself. My brother and the two women escaped to a steamer bound for Ostend.\n\nMeanwhile the curate and I had been buried in a house by the falling fifth cylinder. We were trapped in the kitchen, looking out through a crack at the pit where the Martians worked. I watched the handling-machines, crablike contrivances of glittering metal, and saw the Martians themselves, huge heads with sixteen tentacles, without digestive organs, taking blood from living victims.\n\nThe curate grew weaker and more insane. He ate more than his share of our scant food. He threatened to shout and bring the Martians upon us. When he began screaming scripture at the top of his voice, I struck him with the butt of a meat chopper. He fell dead. A Martian tentacle came through the hole, examined his body, and dragged it away. I hid in the coal cellar for days, starving and terrified.\n\nWhen I finally crawled out, the pit was empty. The Martians had gone. I walked through a world transformed by the red weed, a blood-colored creeper that had choked the rivers and covered the ruins. I found the artilleryman on Putney Hill. He told me the Martians had built a flying-machine and were learning to fly. \"We're down, we're beat,\" he said. \"This isn't a war. It never was a war, any more than there's war between man and ants.\" He had a grandiose plan to live underground, preserve human knowledge, and one day capture a fighting-machine. But I saw he was a dreamer, not a doer. I left him to his cards and champagne and went on alone.\n\nIn dead London I heard a terrible howling from Regent's Park. A Martian stood motionless, crying perpetually. I pushed on towards Primrose Hill. There I found the Martians dead, struck down by the putrefactive bacteria against which their systems had no defense. They had conquered us, but the humblest things on earth had conquered them. I stood on Primrose Hill and thanked God.\n\nI wandered for days, demented. Kind people found me and nursed me back to health. They told me Leatherhead had been destroyed, every soul in it. In despair I returned to my house at Woking. There I found my wife waiting for me, white and tearless. \"I came,\" she said. \"I knew.\" She had escaped with my cousin before the destruction.\n\nNow I sit in my study, writing by lamplight. The Martians are dead, but their machines still stand on Primrose Hill. The world is rebuilding. Yet I cannot shake the fear that they may come again. Mars is still in the sky, and those fiery darts, the shooting stars, will bring with them an unavoidable apprehension to all the sons of men. I hold my wife's hand and think that she has counted me, and I her, among the dead.",
