        'synopsis' => 'A young English solicitor travels to Transylvania to assist a mysterious count, only to discover that his client is a vampire who threatens everyone he loves.',
        'text' => "Jonathan Harker, a young English solicitor, traveled to Transylvania to complete a real estate transaction with Count Dracula. The journey was strange and unsettling. The local peasants crossed themselves and murmured warnings about evil spirits. An old woman gave Jonathan a crucifix for protection. As his carriage climbed through the Borgo Pass, wolves howled all around. A calèche with four black horses met him, driven by a tall man with gleaming red eyes. They arrived at a vast ruined castle just as midnight struck.\n\nCount Dracula was a tall old man with a long white mustache, dressed all in black. He spoke excellent English and welcomed Jonathan warmly. \"Welcome to my house! Enter freely and of your own will!\" His handshake was like ice, and his strength was prodigious. Jonathan noticed the Count had sharp teeth, pointed ears, and hairy palms. He also noticed that the Count never ate or drank, and he cast no reflection in a mirror.\n\nJonathan soon realized he was a prisoner. The doors were locked, and the Count had taken his clothes and papers. Dracula could crawl down the castle walls like a lizard and had power over wolves. One night, three beautiful vampire women appeared in Jonathan's room and tried to kiss him. Dracula drove them away, saying, \"This man belongs to me!\" But Jonathan saw the women take a bag containing a crying child and disappear into the moonlight.\n\nJonathan explored the castle and found the Count lying in a coffin box filled with earth. He tried to kill him with a shovel, but the Count's eyes opened with such hate that Jonathan fled. Soon after, the Szgany gypsies arrived to take the boxes away. Jonathan escaped by climbing down the castle wall, but he fell ill with brain fever and was found by nuns. He wrote frantic letters to his fiancée, Mina, but his memory of the horrors was confused and fragmented.\n\nMeanwhile, Mina Murray wrote to her friend Lucy Westenra. Lucy was beautiful and sweet, and she had three suitors named Arthur Holmwood, Dr. John Seward, and Quincey Morris. She accepted Arthur's proposal, but the other two took it bravely. Lucy's health began to fail. She walked in her sleep and had strange dreams. Mina found her one night on the cliff at Whitby, with something dark bending over her. Two tiny puncture wounds appeared on Lucy's throat.\n\nDr. Seward, who ran a lunatic asylum, was puzzled by Lucy's decline. He called for his old teacher, Professor Abraham Van Helsing, a brilliant Dutch doctor with an open mind. Van Helsing examined Lucy and saw the wounds on her throat. He suspected the truth but did not speak it aloud. He ordered garlic flowers placed around her room and a crucifix put on her neck. But Lucy's mother, meaning well, removed them, and that night Lucy was attacked again. Her mother died of shock, and Lucy's condition worsened.\n\nA transfusion of blood was needed to save Lucy. Arthur Holmwood gave his blood, then Seward, then Van Helsing. But each time, Lucy seemed drained again. Van Helsing realized a vampire was feeding on her. He knew that when Lucy died, she would become one of the Undead.\n\nDespite all efforts, Lucy died. Van Helsing insisted on opening her coffin and found it empty. The next night, it was full again. He knew the terrible truth. The vampire Lucy was feeding on children in Hampstead, leaving tiny puncture wounds on their throats. Van Helsing, Seward, Arthur, and Quincey Morris cornered the vampire in her tomb. Arthur drove a stake through her heart, and Lucy was finally at peace. \"It was my hand that sent her to the stars,\" Van Helsing said.\n\nJonathan Harker had recovered and married Mina. They joined the fight against Dracula. Jonathan gave Mina his Transylvanian journal to read. Van Helsing saw it and confirmed that the Count was the vampire who had attacked Lucy. He had brought fifty boxes of earth to London, each a coffin he could rest in. The boxes were delivered to Carfax, an old house next to Seward's asylum.\n\nRenfield, a patient in Seward's asylum, was a strange maniac who ate flies, spiders, and birds to absorb their life. He worshipped the Count as his \"master.\" When Renfield attacked Seward, he was restrained. He later told them that Dracula had promised him countless lives if he would worship him.\n\nDracula attacked Mina in their home while Jonathan lay in a drugged sleep. He forced her to drink his blood, placing her under his control. Van Helsing put a Sacred Wafer on her forehead, and it burned her flesh like a brand. \"Unclean! Unclean!\" she cried. She was now tied to the vampire in spirit.\n\nVan Helsing organized the hunters. They found twenty-one of the fifty boxes had been moved. They sterilized the remaining earth with pieces of the Host, rendering it useless to Dracula. The Count realized he was cornered. He fled England aboard the ship Czarina Catherine, taking his last box of earth with him.\n\nMina's connection to Dracula became useful. Under hypnosis, she could sense where he was. She reported feeling the lapping of water and creaking of wood. Van Helsing and the hunters followed the Count to Varna, then to Galatz, where they learned the box was being taken up the Sereth River toward the Borgo Pass.\n\nJonathan, Arthur, and Quincey pursued by boat. Seward and Morris rode along the riverbank. Van Helsing took Mina through the Borgo Pass to Dracula's castle. At sunset, the vampire brides appeared and tried to lure Mina, but she was protected by Van Helsing's Holy Circle. Van Helsing entered the castle and destroyed the three vampire women with stakes and knives. He placed a Sacred Wafer in Dracula's tomb, banishing him forever.\n\nThe final chase came at sunset. The Szgany gypsies were carrying Dracula's box on a cart. Jonathan, Arthur, Seward, and Morris caught up to them. Jonathan flung the box to the ground and pried open the lid. As the sun touched the horizon, Dracula's eyes blazed with triumph, but Jonathan plunged his Kukri knife into the vampire's throat while Morris stabbed him through the heart. In an instant, the Count crumbled to dust.\n\nMorris was mortally wounded in the fight. As he lay dying, he saw the sunset glow on Mina's forehead and the scar from the Wafer had vanished. \"Now God be thanked that all has not been in vain! The curse has passed away!\" he said, and died with a smile.\n\nYears later, Jonathan and Mina had a son named Quincey, after their brave friend. They visited Transylvania and saw the castle standing empty and desolate. The terrible days were over, and the world was safe from the vampire. As Van Helsing said, \"We want no proofs, and we ask none to believe us.\"",
