        'cover' => 'covers/classic-three-musketeers.jpg',
        'synopsis' => 'A young Gascon gentleman travels to Paris to join the King\'s Musketeers and becomes entangled in political intrigue, deadly duels, and a treacherous woman\'s vengeance.',
        'text' => "On the first Monday of April 1625, young D'Artagnan left his home in Gascony with fifteen crowns, a yellow horse, and a letter of introduction to Monsieur de Tréville, captain of the King's Musketeers. His father gave him three gifts and some last counsel as well. Endure nothing, he said, from anyone but the king, the cardinal, and Monsieur de Tréville. Fight on all occasions, for a gentleman makes his way by courage alone.\n\nAt the inn in Meung, D'Artagnan's horse was mocked by a stranger with a scar on his temple. The Gascon drew his sword, but the stranger's servants beat him senseless. When D'Artagnan revived, his letter to Tréville had vanished. He sold his horse and walked to Paris, taking a garret room in the Rue des Fossoyeurs.\n\nThe next morning D'Artagnan presented himself at Tréville's hôtel. The captain's antechamber swarmed with Musketeers, among them three inseparable friends named Athos, Porthos, and Aramis. D'Artagnan watched them brawl and jest, hearing them mock the cardinal openly. When Tréville called the three to his office, he scolded them fiercely for a skirmish with the cardinal's Guards. Athos appeared, pale from a wound, and fainted. The captain's stern affection was plain.\n\nD'Artagnan finally gained his audience. He told Tréville of his lost letter and described the man of Meung. Tréville recognized the description and warned the young man to avoid that enemy. He offered D'Artagnan a letter to the Royal Academy and promised to help him. As D'Artagnan left, he saw the man of Meung through the window and rushed after him, but the stranger had vanished.\n\nIn his haste, D'Artagnan collided with Athos's wounded shoulder, then became entangled in Porthos's cloak, then accidentally embarrassed Aramis over a lady's handkerchief. The Gascon found himself with three duels arranged for midday, one o'clock, and two o'clock, against all three Musketeers.\n\nAt the appointed hour, the four met at the Carmes-Deschaux. Before swords could cross, the cardinal's Guards appeared. Jussac demanded the Musketeers surrender. Instead, Athos, Porthos, and Aramis drew their swords. D'Artagnan declared himself a Musketeer at heart and joined them. Outnumbered four against five, they fought brilliantly. D'Artagnan disarmed and wounded Jussac. Athos, Porthos, and Aramis defeated their opponents. The four friends marched back arm in arm, triumphant.\n\nKing Louis XIII, who delighted in tales of fighting, heard the account from Tréville. He summoned the four heroes and gave D'Artagnan forty pistoles. The king admitted that the cardinal would be furious, but he did not care, for he was doing what was right. D'Artagnan was promised a place in the Musketeers after a novitiate.\n\nThe four friends became inseparable. They lived joyously, sharing their purses and their fortunes. D'Artagnan's landlord, Bonacieux, revealed that his wife Constance, the queen's seamstress, had been abducted. The man who took her matched the stranger of Meung. D'Artagnan promised to help. When officers came to arrest Bonacieux, D'Artagnan pretended to abandon him, but later rescued Constance from the cardinal's men. She told him the queen needed a loyal messenger to go to London, and D'Artagnan volunteered.\n\nThe queen, Anne of Austria, had given twelve diamond studs to her lover, the Duke of Buckingham. The cardinal knew this and had arranged for the queen to be asked to wear the studs at an upcoming ball. Two of the studs had been stolen by Milady de Winter, the cardinal's agent. The queen needed the studs returned or her honor would be ruined. D'Artagnan set out for England with his three friends. Along the way they fought through ambushes. Porthos was delayed by a duel, Aramis was wounded, and Athos was arrested. D'Artagnan continued alone, defeating the Comte de Wardes and taking his passport.\n\nIn London, D'Artagnan found Buckingham and delivered the queen's letter. The duke discovered that two studs were missing. He ordered his jeweler to make replacements within two days. Then Buckingham gave D'Artagnan a letter of safe passage and four magnificent horses. D'Artagnan returned to Paris and delivered the studs to the queen just in time. At the ball, the queen appeared with all twelve studs. The cardinal's scheme had failed. The queen rewarded D'Artagnan with a diamond ring.\n\nD'Artagnan now fell under the spell of Milady, the beautiful and dangerous woman who served the cardinal. He pursued her while still searching for Constance, who had disappeared again. Milady played with him cruelly, and D'Artagnan discovered she was the woman Athos had once hanged for being branded with a fleur-de-lis. Milady survived and now wanted revenge against D'Artagnan and all his friends. She sent assassins after him, and she tried to poison him with wine sent as a gift from his friends. Only Athos's caution saved them. D'Artagnan escaped Milady's attempt to kill him, and he learned her terrible secret. She was branded as a criminal.\n\nThe king declared war on the English, and the army marched to La Rochelle. The four friends continued their service. D'Artagnan, now a Musketeer, performed daring feats. He volunteered for a dangerous reconnaissance and discovered two soldiers sent by Milady to kill him. He killed one and captured the other, learning that Milady had been hired by the cardinal to assassinate Buckingham. D'Artagnan's friends helped him secure proof of Milady's crimes.\n\nLord de Winter, Milady's brother-in-law, learned of her treachery from a letter D'Artagnan sent. He had her arrested when she arrived in England and placed her in the care of Lieutenant Felton, a Puritan. Milady seduced Felton with a tale of false persecution, convincing him that Buckingham was a monster who had ruined her. She made Felton believe she was an innocent martyr. Felton fell in love with her and agreed to kill Buckingham. He stabbed the duke at Portsmouth. Buckingham died, and the English fleet never sailed to save La Rochelle.\n\nFelton was captured. Milady escaped during the confusion and fled to France. She took refuge in the Carmelite convent at Béthune, where she found Constance Bonacieux hiding. Milady pretended friendship and offered to help Constance escape. Instead, she poisoned Constance's wine and fled.\n\nThe four friends arrived too late. Constance died in D'Artagnan's arms. Athos consoled him in words that have become famous. \"Women weep for the dead,\" he said. \"Men avenge them.\" Lord de Winter joined them, and together they pursued Milady. They tracked her to a small house by the Lys river. In the house, they held a trial. D'Artagnan accused her of poisoning Constance. Lord de Winter accused her of assassinating Buckingham. Athos accused her of being branded and of ruining his life. The executioner of Lille appeared and told the full story of Milady's crimes. She had been a nun who seduced a priest, stole church valuables, and was branded. The priest, the executioner's brother, hanged himself in prison.\n\nMilady was condemned to death. The executioner bound her and placed her in a boat. On the far bank, he raised his sword. Her head fell. He threw her body into the Lys, saying, \"Let the justice of God be done.\" The four friends returned to Paris.\n\nD'Artagnan was arrested by Rochefort, the cardinal's agent, but he produced the safe-conduct the cardinal himself had given Milady. Richelieu recognized the young man's worth and gave D'Artagnan a lieutenant's commission in the Musketeers. D'Artagnan offered it to Athos, who declined it. Porthos declined it next, hoping to marry a wealthy widow. Aramis declined it too, longing for the quiet of a monastery. D'Artagnan kept the commission and led the Musketeers.\n\nAthos remained a Musketeer under D'Artagnan until 1633, when he retired to a small property. Porthos married Madame Coquenard and enjoyed her fortune. Aramis entered a convent. The four friends remained forever united in memory and honor. D'Artagnan fought Rochefort three times and wounded him each time, and finally they embraced as friends. Planchet became a sergeant. Monsieur Bonacieux lived quietly, never learning the fate of his wife, and vanished one evening, fed and lodged at the expense of the generous cardinal.",
        'questions' => [
