    [
        'slug' => 'classic-monte-cristo',
        'gutenberg_id' => 1184,
        'title' => 'The Count of Monte Cristo',
        'author' => 'Alexandre Dumas',
        'license' => 'Public domain',
        'genres' => ['Adventure'],
        'quiz_difficulty' => 'medium',
        'cover' => 'covers/classic-monte-cristo.jpg',
        'synopsis' => 'A young sailor is falsely imprisoned for years, escapes with a hidden treasure, and returns to Paris as the mysterious Count of Monte Cristo to systematically destroy the men who ruined his life.',
        'text' => "On the 24th of February, 1815, the look-out at Notre-Dame de la Garde signaled the three-master, the Pharaon. The ship drew on and approached the harbor. A young man on board, Edmond Dantes, was fine, tall, and slim, with black eyes and hair as dark as a raven's wing. He was the first mate.\n\n\"Ah, is it you, Dantes?\" cried a man in a skiff. \"What's the matter? Why have you such an air of sadness aboard?\"\n\n\"A great misfortune, Monsieur Morrel,\" replied the young man. \"Off Civita Vecchia we lost our brave Captain Leclere.\"\n\n\"And the cargo?\" inquired the owner eagerly.\n\n\"Is all safe, Monsieur Morrel. But poor Captain Leclere died of brain-fever in dreadful agony.\"\n\nMorrel and Dantes discussed the voyage. Dantes had delivered a packet from the dying captain to Marshal Bertrand on the Island of Elba, and there he had seen the emperor himself. \"He asked me questions about the vessel,\" said Dantes. Morrel was delighted and promised Dantes the captaincy. \"You have managed my affairs so well,\" said Morrel, \"that I ought to allow you all the time you require for your own.\"\n\nDantes went first to see his father, an old man who had grown thin and pale. \"Father, dear father!\" cried Dantes. The old man fell into his arms. Dantes learned that his father had lived on sixty francs for three months after paying a debt to their neighbor Caderousse. \"Heaven pardon me,\" cried Edmond, falling on his knees. He gave his father money and embraced him.\n\nCaderousse arrived, and Dantes greeted him, though coldly. \"We are never quits with those who oblige us,\" said Dantes, \"for when we do not owe them money, we owe them gratitude.\" Caderousse spoke of Mercedes, warning Dantes that fine girls never lack followers. Dantes replied, \"I have a better opinion than you of women in general, and of Mercedes in particular.\"\n\nDantes hurried to the Catalans to see Mercedes. She was a young and beautiful girl with hair as black as jet and eyes like a gazelle's. Fernand, her cousin, a tall young man, was there, pleading with her. \"You see, Mercédès,\" said Fernand, \"here is Easter come round again. Tell me, is this the moment for a wedding?\"\n\n\"I have answered you a hundred times, Fernand,\" she replied. \"I love Edmond Dantes, and none but Edmond shall ever be my husband.\"\n\nWhen Dantes arrived, they embraced. Fernand, pale and trembling, withdrew. Danglars and Caderousse saw him and plotted. Danglars said, \"I should say, for instance, that if after a voyage such as Dantes has just made, in which he touched at the Island of Elba, someone were to denounce him to the king's procureur as a Bonapartist agent.\" Fernand eagerly agreed. Danglars wrote an anonymous letter with his left hand and gave it to Fernand to post. Caderousse, drunk, protested weakly.\n\nThe marriage feast was held at La Reserve. Mercedes sat on Dantes' right, Fernand on her left, pale and agitated. Dantes said, \"Man does not appear to me to be intended to enjoy felicity so unmixed. Happiness is like the enchanted palaces we read of in our childhood, where fierce, fiery dragons defend the entrance.\" Fernand closed his eyes, and a deep groan escaped him.\n\nSuddenly, three blows were struck upon the door. \"I demand admittance,\" said a loud voice, \"in the name of the law!\" A magistrate appeared, followed by soldiers. \"I am the bearer of an order of arrest,\" he said. \"Who among the persons here assembled answers to the name of Edmond Dantes?\"\n\n\"I am he,\" said Dantes. \"What is your pleasure with me?\"\n\n\"Edmond Dantes, I arrest you in the name of the law!\"\n\n\"Me!\" repeated Edmond. \"And wherefore, I pray?\"\n\n\"I cannot inform you, but you will be duly acquainted with the reasons at the preliminary examination.\"\n\nMercedes cried out, \"Adieu, adieu, dearest Edmond!\" The carriage drove off. Morrel hurried after them. Danglars and Caderousse whispered together. \"This, then, I suppose, is a part of the trick you were concerting yesterday?\" said Caderousse. \"All I can say is, if it be so, 'tis an ill turn.\"\n\nVillefort, the deputy procureur, was at his betrothal feast when he was called away to examine the prisoner. He was a young man of twenty-seven, ambitious and proud. He questioned Dantes, who told him the truth about the Elba packet. Villefort saw Dantes was innocent and was about to release him. But when Dantes mentioned the letter was addressed to \"Monsieur Noirtier, Rue Coq-Héron, Paris,\" Villefort started. He recognized his father's name.\n\nHad a thunderbolt fallen at Dantes' feet, he could not have been more transfixed than when Villefort said, \"This letter, which might have ruined me, I will destroy.\" Villefort threw the letter into the fire. \"You see, I destroy it,\" he said. \"You can now have confidence in me after what I have done. Should anyone else interrogate you, say what you have said to me, but do not breathe a word of this letter.\" Dantes promised. Villefort had him secretly taken to the Chateau d'If.\n\nThe Chateau d'If was a gloomy fortress on a rock in the sea. Dantes was thrown into a dungeon. \"Whither are you taking me?\" he asked. \"You will soon know,\" said the gendarme. When Dantes saw the Chateau d'If, he cried, \"The Chateau d'If! What are we going there for?\" The gendarme smiled. \"I am not going there to be imprisoned,\" said Dantes. \"I have committed no crime.\" \"All the formalities have been gone through,\" said the gendarme. Dantes tried to throw himself into the sea, but the guards seized him. He was dragged into the fortress.\n\nFor fourteen years, Dantes rotted in the Chateau d'If. He passed through despair and madness. One night, he heard a hollow sound in the wall. \"Who talks of God and despair at the same time?\" said a voice. It was the Abbé Faria, an old priest who had been digging an escape tunnel. They became friends. Faria educated Dantes in languages, history, and science. \"I have nearly five thousand volumes in my library at Rome,\" said Faria, \"but I found that with one hundred and fifty well-chosen books a man possesses all that a man need really know.\"\n\nFaria also told Dantes of a treasure hidden on the Island of Monte Cristo. \"The Cardinal Spada buried it there,\" said Faria. \"Two millions of Roman crowns. Nearly thirteen millions of our money.\" Dantes could hardly believe it. \"This treasure belongs to you, my dear friend,\" said Dantes. \"I have no right to it.\" \"You are my son, Dantes,\" said Faria. \"You are the child of my captivity.\"\n\nFaria suffered a final attack of catalepsy and died. Dantes sewed himself into the corpse's shroud. When the jailers threw the sack into the sea, he cut himself free and swam to a deserted island. He cried out, \"I am saved!\" He was rescued by a smuggling vessel and eventually found the treasure, a coffer filled with gold ingots, diamonds, and pearls. \"Was he awake, or was it but a dream?\" He fell on his knees and prayed.\n\nDantes became the Count of Monte Cristo, immensely wealthy and powerful. He returned to the world a changed man, pale and cold, with revenge his only purpose. He learned that his father had died of starvation, Mercedes had married Fernand (now the Count of Morcerf), Danglars was a rich banker, and Villefort was the chief prosecutor in Paris.\n\nHe began with Caderousse, now a poor innkeeper. Disguised as the Abbé Busoni, he gave Caderousse a diamond. \"Here, my friend, take this diamond, it is yours,\" said the abbé. \"But in exchange, give me the red silk purse that Monsieur Morrel left on old Dantes' chimney-piece.\" Caderousse, greedy, later killed his wife and the jeweler to possess the diamond. He was arrested and sent to prison.\n\nIn Rome, the Count met Albert de Morcerf and Franz d'Epinay. He engineered a rescue when Albert was captured by the bandit Luigi Vampa. Albert was grateful and invited the Count to Paris. \"I shall be at your house on the 21st of May at half-past ten in the morning,\" said the Count.\n\nIn Paris, Monte Cristo became a sensation. He gave away horses worth thirty-two thousand francs. He saved Madame de Villefort and her son from a carriage accident. With a simple bribe he manipulated a telegraph operator, telling him, \"Do this, and you will have nectarines and all the rest.\" The false message made Danglars lose a fortune.\n\nHe introduced a false father and son, the \"Major\" and \"Andrea\" Cavalcanti, to Paris. Andrea was in fact Benedetto, the child Villefort had buried alive, now a criminal. He encouraged Andrea to pursue marriage with Danglars' daughter, Eugenie. \"When one is going to be married, there is nothing like implicit confidence,\" said Caderousse to Andrea, not knowing the truth.\n\nMonte Cristo brought Haydee, the daughter of Ali Pasha, to Paris. At the right moment, she testified before the Chamber of Peers, revealing Fernand's treachery. \"I am Haydée, the daughter of Ali Tepelini, pasha of Yanina!\" she cried. \"You are Fernand Mondego, the French officer who led the troops of my noble father! It is you who surrendered the castle of Yanina! It is you who sold us, my mother and me!\" Fernand, exposed, fled in disgrace.\n\nAlbert de Morcerf challenged Monte Cristo to a duel. But Mercedes came to the Count and begged for her son's life. \"Edmond, you will not kill my son!\" she cried. \"Mercédès is dead, madame,\" said Monte Cristo. \"I know no one now of that name.\" But Mercedes persisted. \"Edmond, if you knew all the prayers I have addressed to God for you!\" At last, Monte Cristo relented. \"He shall live,\" he said. \"I will make him suffer no more.\" Fernand shot himself after his wife and son left him.\n\nVillefort suffered the most terrible fate. During Andrea's trial, the young man revealed that he was Villefort's son. \"My father is king's attorney,\" said Andrea calmly. \"He is named Villefort.\" Then Villefort's wife, who had been systematically poisoning her family, poisoned herself and her child. Villefort, driven mad, rushed to his garden, digging frantically. \"I will find him!\" he cried. \"I will find him, though I dig forever!\" He lost his mind entirely.\n\nDanglars was ruined financially. He fled Paris with his remaining money, was captured by Luigi Vampa, and held in a cave. He was forced to pay exorbitant sums for each meal until he was nearly starved. \"Are you a Christian?\" he begged. \"Do you wish to assassinate a man who is a brother?\" At last, Monte Cristo revealed himself. \"I am he whom you sold and dishonored,\" said the Count. \"I am Edmond Dantes!\" Danglars fell prostrate. \"I forgive you,\" said the Count. \"Your life is safe.\" Danglars rose, his hair turned white.\n\nIn his quest for vengeance, Monte Cristo had nearly destroyed Maximilian Morrel, the son of his old benefactor, who loved Valentine de Villefort. The Count saved her life and reunited the lovers. To Maximilian he sent words that have become famous. \"There is neither happiness nor misery in the world. There is only the comparison of one state with another. All human wisdom is summed up in two words, Wait and Hope.\"\n\nHaydee declared she would rather die than leave the Count. \"Do you then love me?\" he asked. \"Oh, yes,\" she cried, \"I do love you! I love you as one loves a father, brother, husband!\" With his vengeance complete, Monte Cristo sailed away from Paris, finding peace with Haydee, leaving behind the city where he had enacted his terrible plan.",
        'questions' => [
            ['section' => 'Vocabulary', 'type' => 'mcq', 'question_text' => 'What does "captivity" mean as Faria uses it about his years?', 'option_a' => 'The state of being held prisoner', 'option_b' => 'A long sea voyage', 'option_c' => 'A period of great wealth', 'option_d' => 'Time spent studying books', 'correct_option' => 'a'],
            ['section' => 'Vocabulary', 'type' => 'identification', 'question_text' => 'Which word names the cloth wrapped around a dead body for burial? Dantes hid himself inside one.', 'acceptable_answers' => ['shroud']],
