    [
        'slug' => 'classic-tom-sawyer',
        'gutenberg_id' => 74,
        'title' => 'The Adventures of Tom Sawyer',
        'author' => 'Mark Twain',
        'license' => 'Public domain',
        'genres' => ['Adventure'],
        'quiz_difficulty' => 'easy',
        'cover' => 'covers/classic-tom-sawyer.jpg',
        'synopsis' => 'A mischievous boy in a small Mississippi River town gets into endless trouble, witnesses a murder, and hunts for buried treasure with his best friend.',
        'text' => "Tom Sawyer lived with his Aunt Polly in the small town of St. Petersburg, Missouri. He was always up to some mischief, whether it was stealing jam, playing hooky from school, or fighting with strange boys. Aunt Polly loved him dearly but struggled to keep him in line. She was forever threatening to whip him, though her heart was never really in it.\n\nOne Saturday morning, Aunt Polly made Tom whitewash the fence as punishment for his misdeeds. The task seemed hopeless until Tom hit upon a brilliant plan. When his friend Ben Rogers came by, Tom pretended that whitewashing was a privilege, not a chore. Ben asked, \"Say, Tom, let me whitewash a little.\" Tom replied, \"No, I reckon it wouldn't hardly do, Ben. You see, Aunt Polly's awful particular about this fence. There ain't one boy in a thousand, maybe two thousand, that can do it the way it's got to be done.\" Before long, all the boys in the village had traded their treasures to Tom for the honor of whitewashing the fence. Tom sat in the shade, collecting marbles, kites, and assorted trinkets, while the fence received three coats of whitewash. He had discovered a great law of human action. To make someone covet a thing, make it difficult to attain.\n\nTom fell in love at first sight with Becky Thatcher, the new girl in town. He showed off shamelessly to win her admiration. At school he traded his treasures for tickets that earned him a Bible, which he received before the whole school. But when Judge Thatcher asked him to name the first two disciples, Tom proudly answered, \"David and Goliah!\" to everyone's mortification. Becky was impressed anyway.\n\nIn church one Sunday, Tom distracted the congregation with a pinchbug that bit a poodle dog, sending it yelping through the aisle. The sermon was ruined, but Tom enjoyed every moment.\n\nTom and his friend Huckleberry Finn, the town outcast, went to the graveyard at midnight with a dead cat to cure warts. They witnessed three grave robbers, Muff Potter, Injun Joe, and young Doctor Robinson, digging up a body. A fight broke out. Injun Joe stabbed the doctor and then knocked Potter unconscious. When Potter woke, Injun Joe convinced him he had done the murder. \"I didn't do it!\" Potter cried. \"I never done it!\" But Joe told him, \"You two was scuffling, and you snatched the knife and jammed it into him.\" The boys fled in terror and swore a blood oath never to tell what they had seen.\n\nPotter was arrested and jailed. Tom's conscience tormented him. He smuggled food and comforts to the prisoner, but he could not bring himself to speak the truth. Injun Joe testified against Potter in court, and everyone believed the poor drunkard was guilty.\n\nTom, Joe Harper, and Huck decided to run away and become pirates. They stole a raft and landed on Jackson's Island in the Mississippi River. They lived like outlaws, swimming, fishing, and playing at being Indians. But after a few days, they grew homesick. Tom sneaked back to town one night and hid under his aunt's bed while she and Mrs. Harper mourned their lost boys. He learned that the villagers planned to hold a funeral for them on Sunday.\n\nThe next morning, Tom returned to camp and told his friends his plan. On Sunday, the three pirates marched into the church during their own funeral service. The congregation was astonished, and Tom basked in the glory of his triumphant return.\n\nTom's fame grew, but his troubles did not end. Becky stopped coming to school, and Tom moped around the house. Aunt Polly tried all sorts of remedies to cure his gloom, including Pain-killer, which Tom secretly fed to the cat. The cat went wild, and Aunt Polly finally realized Tom had been deceiving him.\n\nWhen Becky returned to school, Tom and she had a falling out over his former sweetheart, Amy Lawrence. In revenge, Becky began paying attention to Alfred Temple. Tom was consumed with jealousy. Later, Becky accidentally tore the teacher's anatomy book. When the teacher demanded to know who did it, Tom took the blame and received a terrible whipping. \"Tom, how could you be so noble!\" Becky whispered to him afterward. She forgave him everything.\n\nThe murder trial began. Tom could bear the burden of his secret no longer. He testified in court, describing exactly what he had seen that night in the graveyard. \"And as the doctor fetched the board around and Muff Potter fell, Injun Joe jumped with the knife!\" Before he could finish, Injun Joe leaped through a window and escaped. Muff Potter was set free, and Tom became the village hero. But his nights were now haunted by fear of Injun Joe's revenge.\n\nIn the weeks that followed, Tom and Huck took up treasure hunting. Their search led them to an old haunted house. While hiding upstairs, they overheard Injun Joe and another man talking below. The men found a chest of gold coins buried in the house and decided to move it to a safer place, which they called \"Number Two.\" Tom and Huck resolved to track them and recover the treasure.\n\nThe town went on a picnic to McDougal's Cave. Tom and Becky wandered away from the group and became hopelessly lost in the labyrinth of passages. Their candles burned down, and they were left in total darkness. Days passed. They grew weak from hunger and thirst. Tom explored side passages with a kite string to find a way out. In one passage, he encountered Injun Joe himself, but the half-breed fled into the darkness.\n\nMeanwhile, the town searched frantically. Huck, recovering from a fever, had been watching the Temperance Tavern where he suspected the treasure was hidden. One night he saw Injun Joe and his partner heading toward the Widow Douglas's house. Huck ran to the Welshman's house for help. \"Please don't ever tell I told you,\" Huck begged. \"I'd be killed, sure.\" The Welshman and his sons chased the would-be attackers away, saving the widow's life. Huck fell ill again, and the widow nursed him tenderly.\n\nAfter three days, Tom found an opening that led to daylight. He and Becky were rescued. The village rejoiced. Judge Thatcher ordered the cave door sealed with boiler iron to prevent anyone else from getting lost.\n\nWhen Tom learned the cave had been sealed, he remembered that Injun Joe was still inside. \"Oh, Judge, Injun Joe's in the cave!\" Tom cried. The men returned and found the half-breed dead, starved to death, with his face pressed against the crack of the door. Tom felt pity for the wretch, but also a profound relief that his enemy was gone.\n\nTom and Huck returned to the cave through a secret entrance Tom had discovered. Following the mark of a cross on the wall, they found the treasure chest under a rock. \"Got it at last!\" Huck said, plowing among the coins with his hand. It contained over twelve thousand dollars in gold. They hauled it out and hid it in the widow's woodshed.\n\nThat evening, the widow gave a party in honor of the Welshman and his sons. Tom and Huck arrived late and filthy, but the widow insisted they get cleaned up and join the festivities. When Tom revealed the treasure chest and dumped the gold on the table, everyone was stunned. \"Huck don't need it,\" Tom declared. \"Huck's rich.\"\n\nThe money was invested for the boys. Tom had a dollar and a quarter a week, enough to live like a prince. Huck was taken in by the Widow Douglas and forced to wear clean clothes, go to school, and sleep in a proper bed. He hated every minute of it. \"It ain't for me,\" he told Tom. \"I can't stand them ways. She makes me get up just at the same time every morning. She makes me wash. They comb me all to thunder. I got to wear them blamed clothes that just smothers me.\"\n\nTom found him and persuaded him to give civilization another try. \"Huck, being rich ain't going to keep me back from turning robber,\" Tom said. \"But we can't let you into the gang if you ain't respectable.\" Huck agreed to go back to the widow for a month if Tom would let him join the gang. \"Well, I'll go back to the widder for a month and tackle it,\" Huck said, \"and see if I can come to stand it, if you'll let me b'long to the gang, Tom.\" Tom's adventures were far from over, but that was a story for another day.",
        'questions' => [
            ['section' => 'Vocabulary', 'type' => 'mcq', 'question_text' => 'What does "whitewash" mean?', 'option_a' => 'Paint a wall or fence white', 'option_b' => 'Wash clothes in a river', 'option_c' => 'Clean up after a fight', 'option_d' => 'Scrub a floor until it shines', 'correct_option' => 'a'],
            ['section' => 'Vocabulary', 'type' => 'identification', 'question_text' => 'Which word means playful troublemaking? Tom was famous for it.', 'acceptable_answers' => ['mischief']],
