    [
        'slug' => 'classic-wizard-oz',
        'gutenberg_id' => 55,
        'title' => 'The Wizard of Oz',
        'author' => 'L. Frank Baum',
        'license' => 'Public domain',
        'genres' => ['Fantasy'],
        'quiz_difficulty' => 'easy',
        'cover' => 'covers/classic-wizard-oz.jpg',
        'synopsis' => 'A Kansas girl is carried by a cyclone to a magical land, where she must find her way home with the help of three unusual companions.',
        'text' => "Dorothy lived in the midst of the great Kansas prairies with Uncle Henry and Aunt Em. Their house was small and gray, like everything else on the dreary plain. Aunt Em had once been young and pretty, but the sun and wind had taken the sparkle from her eyes and the red from her cheeks. Uncle Henry worked hard from morning till night and never laughed. Only Toto, Dorothy's little black dog, kept her from growing as gray as her surroundings.\n\nOne day a cyclone came. Uncle Henry ran to check the livestock. Aunt Em screamed, \"Quick, Dorothy! Run for the cellar!\" But Toto jumped under the bed, and by the time Dorothy caught him, the house was already rising through the air. It whirled around and rose higher and higher, carried miles and miles away like a feather. Dorothy rode quite easily, rocked like a baby in a cradle. At last she fell asleep on her bed with Toto beside her.\n\nA sudden shock awakened her. The house had landed gently in a country of marvelous beauty. Lovely patches of greensward, stately trees with rich fruits, and banks of gorgeous flowers surrounded her. A group of odd-looking people came toward her, about as tall as Dorothy, wearing round hats with little bells and clothes of blue. The little old woman among them wore white and walked stiffly. She bowed low and said, \"You are welcome, most noble Sorceress, to the land of the Munchkins. We are grateful to you for killing the Wicked Witch of the East and setting our people free.\"\n\nDorothy was astonished. She had never killed anything. But the little woman pointed to the corner of the house, where two feet in silver shoes with pointed toes were sticking out from under a beam. The Witch of the East had ruled the Munchkins for years, making them slaves. Now she was dead, dried up by the sun until nothing remained but the silver shoes. The little woman was the Witch of the North, a good witch. She told Dorothy there were four witches in the Land of Oz, two good and two wicked. Dorothy's house had killed one wicked witch, and only the Wicked Witch of the West remained.\n\nDorothy wanted to go home to Kansas. The Witch of the North told her the only hope was to go to the Emerald City and ask the Great Wizard Oz for help. She kissed Dorothy on the forehead, leaving a round, shining mark to protect her. \"The road to the City of Emeralds is paved with yellow brick,\" she said, \"so you cannot miss it.\"\n\nDorothy put on the silver shoes, packed a basket, and started down the yellow brick road with Toto. She passed through the beautiful country of the Munchkins, where everyone bowed to her for destroying the Wicked Witch. At night she stayed with a rich Munchkin named Boq, who was amazed by her silver shoes.\n\nThe next day she came upon a Scarecrow perched on a pole in a cornfield. To her surprise, it winked at her. \"Good day,\" said the Scarecrow. She lifted him down, and he told her he had no brains and wanted to ask Oz for some. \"There is only one thing in the world I am afraid of,\" he said. \"It's a lighted match.\" Dorothy invited him to come along.\n\nThey traveled through rougher country. The Scarecrow stumbled often, but it never hurt him. He told Dorothy his story, how the farmer had painted his eyes and ears, and how an old crow had told him brains were the only things worth having. Dorothy said there was no place like home, and the Scarecrow could not understand why she would leave such a beautiful country.\n\nIn the forest they found a Tin Woodman, rusted solid with an axe raised in his hands. He had been groaning for more than a year. Dorothy oiled his joints with an oil-can from his cottage. The Tin Woodman told his story. The Wicked Witch of the East had enchanted his axe so it cut off his legs, then his arms, then his head, then split his body in two. Each time a tinsmith replaced the lost parts with tin. But when he lost his heart, he lost his love for the beautiful Munchkin girl he had planned to marry. Now he wanted Oz to give him a heart. He joined the party.\n\nThey walked on until a great Lion bounded into the road. He knocked the Scarecrow spinning and struck at the Tin Woodman, but his claws could not dent the tin. When the Lion tried to bite Toto, Dorothy slapped his nose and called him a coward. \"I know it,\" said the Lion, hanging his head in shame. \"I've always known it. But how can I help it?\" He wanted Oz to give him courage. He joined them, and soon Toto and the Lion became good friends.\n\nThey crossed a wide ditch by having the Lion jump over with each of them on his back. Then they came to an even wider ditch. The Tin Woodman chopped down a tree to make a bridge, but two Kalidahs, beasts with bear bodies and tiger heads, chased them. The Scarecrow had the Woodman chop the tree so the Kalidahs fell into the ditch and were dashed to pieces on the rocks below.\n\nA broad river blocked their path. The Woodman built a raft, but the current swept it downstream. The Scarecrow got stuck on a pole in the river, and the Lion swam to shore pulling the raft. A kind Stork rescued the Scarecrow. They continued through a meadow of scarlet poppies. Dorothy, Toto, and the Lion fell asleep from the deadly scent. The Scarecrow and the Tin Woodman, being made of straw and tin, carried Dorothy out of the poppy field. The Queen of the Field Mice and her thousands of subjects, grateful to the Woodman for killing a wildcat that had chased her, helped drag the Lion out on a truck made of wooden pegs.\n\nThey reached the Emerald City at last. The Guardian of the Gates gave them green spectacles to wear so the brightness of the city would not blind them. They entered the beautiful city, where everything was green. Oz agreed to see them, but only one each day.\n\nDorothy went first. She saw an enormous Head on a throne. It said she must kill the Wicked Witch of the West before Oz would send her home. The Scarecrow saw a lovely Lady, the Tin Woodman saw a terrible Beast, and the Lion saw a Ball of Fire. Each was told the same thing. Kill the Witch, and their wishes would be granted.\n\nThey set off for the land of the West. The Wicked Witch saw them coming through her one powerful eye. She sent forty wolves, but the Tin Woodman chopped all their heads off. She sent forty wild crows, but the Scarecrow twisted all their necks. She sent a swarm of black bees, but the Scarecrow had the Woodman scatter his straw over Dorothy and the Lion while the bees broke their stings against the tin. Finally, the Witch used her last resource, the Golden Cap, which commanded the Winged Monkeys.\n\nThe Winged Monkeys carried the Tin Woodman to sharp rocks and dropped him, battered and dented. They pulled the straw out of the Scarecrow and threw his clothes into a tall tree. They tied the Lion with ropes and took him to the Witch's castle. But they dared not harm Dorothy because of the Good Witch's kiss on her forehead. They set her down at the Witch's doorstep.\n\nThe Wicked Witch was afraid of Dorothy's silver shoes. She made Dorothy work in the kitchen, and by a trick stole one of the shoes. In anger, Dorothy threw a bucket of water over the Witch. The Witch began to melt and shrink away. \"See what you have done!\" she screamed. \"In a minute I shall melt away.\" She cried out that water would be the end of her, and soon nothing was left but a brown, shapeless mass. Dorothy was free.\n\nShe released the Lion and called the Winkies, the Witch's slaves, to help her. They carried the Tin Woodman back to the castle, where tinsmiths straightened him out and mended all his dents. They found the Scarecrow's clothes in a tree, restuffed him with clean straw, and he was as good as new. The Winkies begged the Tin Woodman to stay and rule them. Dorothy found the Golden Cap in the Witch's cupboard and took it.\n\nOn the journey back, they lost their way. Dorothy called the Field Mice, and the Queen told her the Golden Cap held the charm to summon the Winged Monkeys. Dorothy spoke the magic words, and the Monkeys carried them to the Emerald City.\n\nWhen they demanded to see Oz, they found no one in the Throne Room. A Voice told them he was invisible. But Toto knocked over a screen, revealing a little old man. \"I am Oz, the Great and Terrible,\" he said in a trembling voice. He confessed he was a humbug, not a wizard at all. He had come from Omaha in a balloon and had ruled the Emerald City by deception. He had made the Head, the Lady, the Beast, and the Ball of Fire with ventriloquism and tricks.\n\nThe Scarecrow was dismayed, but Oz gave him a head full of bran and pins and needles to make him feel wise. He gave the Tin Woodman a silk heart stuffed with sawdust. He gave the Lion a green liquid to drink, telling him it was courage. To Dorothy, he promised to take her back to Kansas in a balloon. They sewed the balloon together, and Oz climbed into the basket. \"Come, Dorothy!\" he cried. \"Hurry up, or the balloon will fly away.\" But Toto ran into the crowd chasing a kitten, and Dorothy ran after him. The ropes broke, and the balloon rose without her. Oz sailed away and was never seen again.\n\nThe Scarecrow became ruler of the Emerald City. Dorothy still longed to go home. The Winged Monkeys could not carry her across the desert, so she decided to travel to the South and ask Glinda, the Good Witch of the South. Her companions all agreed to go with her.\n\nThey traveled through a forest with fighting trees that bent down to seize them until the Tin Woodman chopped them. They crossed a wall into the Dainty China Country, where everything was made of china and very brittle. The Lion accidentally broke a church with his tail. They apologized to the china Princess and moved on.\n\nIn a great forest, the Lion found hundreds of beasts holding a meeting. A monstrous spider with a body like an elephant and legs like tree trunks had been eating them. The Lion fought and killed the spider, and the beasts bowed down to him as their King. He promised to return and rule them after Dorothy was safe.\n\nThey came to a hill guarded by the Hammer-Heads, strange armless men with heads that shot out and knocked travelers down the hill. They could not pass until Dorothy used her last command of the Golden Cap to summon the Winged Monkeys. The Monkeys carried them over the hill to the country of the Quadlings.\n\nAt Glinda's ruby castle, the Good Witch welcomed them. She told Dorothy the silver shoes could carry her anywhere in three steps. \"All you have to do is knock the heels together three times and command the shoes to carry you wherever you wish to go,\" said Glinda. Dorothy hugged her friends goodbye, kissed the Lion and the Tin Woodman, and hugged the Scarecrow. She took Toto in her arms, knocked her heels together, and said, \"Take me home to Aunt Em!\"\n\nIn an instant she was whirling through the air. The shoes took three steps, and she landed on the Kansas prairie, not far from the new farmhouse Uncle Henry had built. Her silver shoes had fallen off in the desert and were lost forever. Aunt Em came out to water the cabbages and saw Dorothy running toward her. \"My darling child!\" she cried, folding the little girl in her arms. Dorothy told her she had come from the Land of Oz, and said, \"Oh, Aunt Em! I'm so glad to be at home again!\"",
        'questions' => [
            ['section' => 'Vocabulary', 'type' => 'mcq', 'question_text' => 'What is a "cyclone"?', 'option_a' => 'A violent spinning windstorm', 'option_b' => 'A heavy summer rain', 'option_c' => 'A bright flash of lightning', 'option_d' => 'A cold winter blizzard', 'correct_option' => 'a'],
            ['section' => 'Vocabulary', 'type' => 'identification', 'question_text' => 'Which word means someone who deceives people with tricks? Oz admitted being one.', 'acceptable_answers' => ['humbug']],
            ['section' => 'Comprehension', 'type' => 'true_false', 'question_text' => 'A bucket of water melts the Wicked Witch of the West.', 'correct_option' => 'a'],
            ['section' => 'Comprehension', 'type' => 'sequence', 'question_text' => 'Put the events of the story in order.', 'sequence_items' => ['A cyclone carries Dorothy\'s house to the land of the Munchkins', 'She frees the Scarecrow, oils the Tin Woodman and befriends the Lion', 'Each traveler asks Oz for help and is told to kill the witch', 'A bucket of water melts the Wicked Witch of the West', 'Glinda reveals the silver shoes can carry Dorothy home']],
            ['section' => 'Comprehension', 'type' => 'matching', 'question_text' => 'Match each figure to their wish.', 'match_pairs' => [['left' => 'Scarecrow', 'right' => 'Wants brains from the great Wizard'], ['left' => 'Tin Woodman', 'right' => 'Wants a heart so he can love again'], ['left' => 'Cowardly Lion', 'right' => 'Wants courage above all things'], ['left' => 'Glinda', 'right' => 'Good witch who knows the secret of the silver shoes']]],
            ['section' => 'Comprehension', 'type' => 'mcq', 'question_text' => 'Why could the Winged Monkeys not harm Dorothy?', 'option_a' => 'The Good Witch\'s kiss on her forehead protected her', 'option_b' => 'Her silver shoes blinded their eyes', 'option_c' => 'Toto bit every monkey that came near', 'option_d' => 'They feared the power of Oz', 'correct_option' => 'a'],
            ['section' => 'Comprehension', 'type' => 'mcq', 'question_text' => 'How does Dorothy finally get home to Kansas?', 'option_a' => 'She knocks her heels together three times and commands the shoes', 'option_b' => 'Oz flies her back in his balloon', 'option_c' => 'The Winged Monkeys carry her across the desert', 'option_d' => 'She follows the yellow brick road backward', 'correct_option' => 'a'],
            ['section' => 'Comprehension', 'type' => 'identification', 'question_text' => 'What is the name of Dorothy\'s little dog?', 'acceptable_answers' => ['toto']],
