    [
        'slug' => 'classic-secret-garden',
        'gutenberg_id' => 113,
        'title' => 'The Secret Garden',
        'author' => 'Frances Hodgson Burnett',
        'license' => 'Public domain',
        'genres' => ['Realistic Fiction'],
        'quiz_difficulty' => 'easy',
        'cover' => 'covers/classic-secret-garden.jpg',
        'synopsis' => 'A sour, lonely girl discovers a hidden garden and, with new friends, brings it back to life, and in doing so heals herself and the sickly boy who lives in the great house.',
        'text' => "Mary Lennox was the most disagreeable-looking child anyone had ever seen. She had a thin little face, thin yellow hair, and a sour expression. She had been born in India, where her mother, a beautiful socialite, had not wanted her at all. Mary was raised by native servants who gave her everything she wanted, and she grew into a selfish, tyrannical little girl.\n\nOne day, cholera broke out in the bungalow. Everyone died or fled. Mary, forgotten in the nursery, woke to find the house empty and silent. She was discovered by soldiers and sent to England to live with her uncle, Mr. Archibald Craven, at Misselthwaite Manor on the Yorkshire moors.\n\nThe journey was long and dreary. Mary's uncle was a hunchback who had shut himself away after his wife died ten years ago. The house was huge and gloomy, with a hundred rooms, most of them locked. Mary was given rooms of her own and told to keep out of the way. Mrs. Medlock, the housekeeper, warned her not to wander.\n\nMary's servant, Martha, was a talkative Yorkshire girl with a warm heart. Martha told Mary about the moor and about her brother Dickon, who could charm wild animals. She also mentioned that there was a garden that had been locked up for ten years ever since Mrs. Craven died. Mary became intensely curious about it.\n\nThe next day, Mary went outside. She met Ben Weatherstaff, a surly old gardener, and his friend, a robin redbreast. The robin was tame and curious, and Mary felt a strange connection to him. When Ben told her that the secret garden had been Mrs. Craven's favorite place, Mary became determined to find it.\n\nMartha told Mary about Dickon, who was kind and could talk to animals. Mary wrote Dickon a letter asking him to buy her some garden tools and seeds. Martha took the letter home on her day off.\n\nMary wandered the gardens and one day saw the robin hopping near a patch of freshly turned earth. She found an old key half-buried in the soil. She wondered if it was the key to the secret garden. The robin then led her to a spot where the ivy hung loose, and Mary found a hidden door. The key fit the lock. She pushed the door open and stepped inside.\n\nThe garden was beautiful and wild, overgrown with tangled roses and ivy. It was silent and still, as if it had been waiting for her. Mary began to work, digging up weeds and clearing space around the bulbs she found. She felt happier than she had ever felt in her life.\n\nMartha brought Mary a skipping rope from her mother. Mary began to skip outside every day, which made her stronger and gave her an appetite. Her health improved dramatically. She also wrote a letter to Dickon ordering garden tools and seeds. Dickon brought them himself. He was a wonderful boy with round blue eyes, red cheeks, and a charming way with animals. He brought his tame crow, Soot, and a fox cub named Captain.\n\nMary showed Dickon the secret garden. He was amazed and promised to help her bring it back to life. They worked together, weeding, pruning, and planting. The garden began to come alive with green shoots and buds.\n\nMary heard crying in the house at night. Martha tried to pretend it was the wind, but Mary knew better. One night, she followed the sound and found a boy named Colin lying in a bed in a hidden room. He had huge gray eyes, a thin face, and a terrible temper. He was Mary's cousin and Mr. Craven's son. Everyone believed he was going to die.\n\nColin was a spoiled, frightened boy who had been told he had a weak back and would grow into a hunchback. He refused to let anyone look at him. But he was interested in Mary and the secret garden. He made Mary promise to come and talk to him every day.\n\nMary told Colin about Dickon and the moor and the garden. Colin became fascinated by the idea of a secret place no one knew about. He decided he wanted to see the garden and that it must remain a secret. Mary was afraid to tell him too much, but she began to trust him.\n\nMary asked Mr. Craven if she could have a bit of earth to garden. He agreed absent-mindedly and told her she might do as she liked. He also said that Susan Sowerby, Martha's mother, was right about Mary needing fresh air and freedom.\n\nMary's visits to Colin became regular. She told him about Dickon's animals and the spring coming to the garden. Colin began to change. He ate more, laughed, and forgot about dying. Dr. Craven was amazed and puzzled. Mrs. Medlock warned Mary to be careful, but Mary was not afraid of Colin.\n\nDickon brought his animals to visit Colin. The lamb, the fox, the crow, and the squirrels charmed Colin completely. He decided to try standing up. With Mary's encouragement and Dickon's help, Colin stood on his feet for the first time. He was not a hunchback! He had been lying to himself and letting others lie to him.\n\nColin made a plan. He would keep his recovery a secret until his father came home. Then he would walk into his father's study and show him he was well. Mary and Dickon agreed to help him keep the secret.\n\nEvery day, Colin was taken out to the garden in his chair. Dickon pushed him, and Mary walked alongside. When they reached the secret garden, Colin got out of his chair and walked. He walked more each day. His legs grew stronger. He began doing exercises Dickon taught him.\n\nThe garden continued to bloom. Roses climbed the walls, flowers burst from the earth, and birds nested everywhere. The three children worked together, planting and weeding. Mary and Colin grew healthier and happier. They even began to sing the Doxology.\n\nBen Weatherstaff discovered the children in the garden and was furious at first. Then he saw Colin standing and walking. He was amazed and moved to tears. He promised to keep their secret and began to help them in the garden.\n\nColin became an expert on \"Magic.\" He lectured the others about how thinking positive thoughts could make you strong. He believed that the Magic in the garden was what had healed him. They performed rituals under the trees, chanting to the Magic.\n\nSusan Sowerby visited the garden and met Colin and Mary. She was warm and wise, and she told Colin that his mother would be proud of him. Colin felt as if he had found a real mother. He told her he would walk into his father's study when Mr. Craven returned.\n\nMeanwhile, Mr. Craven had been traveling abroad. He was still grieving his wife, but strange things began to happen. The dream he had been running from began to feel less heavy. A letter from Susan Sowerby arrived, urging him to come home.\n\nMr. Craven returned to Misselthwaite. He walked through the grounds and heard laughter coming from the secret garden. The door was open. As he stood there, a boy burst through the ivy, almost running into him.\n\nIt was Colin, healthy, strong, and laughing. He was not a hunchback. He was a handsome boy with his mother's eyes. \"I'm Colin,\" he said. \"I'm going to live forever and ever and ever.\"\n\nMr. Craven was overwhelmed with joy. He held his son and walked with him through the garden. Colin showed him everything, the roses, the trees, the new plants, the birds. The garden that had been dead for ten years was now alive with color and life.\n\nThe servants were astonished when they saw Colin walking across the lawn with his father. Mrs. Medlock nearly fainted. But everyone was overjoyed. Colin had been saved, and so had Mr. Craven.\n\nMary had found a home, a family, and a purpose. She had gone from being a sour, selfish child to a healthy, happy girl who loved people and gardens. The secret garden had healed them all, Mary, Colin, and even Mr. Craven.",
        'questions' => [
            ['section' => 'Vocabulary', 'type' => 'mcq', 'question_text' => 'What does "disagreeable" mean?', 'option_a' => 'Unpleasant and hard to like', 'option_b' => 'Very beautiful', 'option_c' => 'Always ready to argue in court', 'option_d' => 'Too tired to walk', 'correct_option' => 'a'],
