        'cover' => 'covers/classic-jungle-book.jpg',
        'synopsis' => 'A boy raised by wolves in the Indian jungle must face his greatest enemy, the tiger Shere Khan, and decide where he truly belongs, with the beasts or with men.',
        'text' => "One warm evening in the Seeonee hills, Father Wolf woke from his day's rest and prepared to hunt. Tabaqui the jackal came to the cave, bringing news that Shere Khan the tiger had shifted his hunting grounds. Shere Khan was lame in one foot and had taken to killing cattle, and now the villagers were angry. Soon after, Father Wolf heard the tiger's purring hum, the sound that meant he was hunting Man.\n\nA woodcutter's campfire had frightened Shere Khan, and he had burned his feet. But a man's cub, a naked brown baby, had wandered into the wolves' cave. Father Wolf brought the child to Mother Wolf, who named him Mowgli, \"the Frog,\" and declared he would be her son. When Shere Khan demanded the cub, Mother Wolf stood fierce and defiant. \"The man's cub is mine, Lungri!\" she cried. \"He shall not be killed. He shall live to run with the Pack and hunt with the Pack. Now get hence, burned beast of the jungle!\"\n\nFather Wolf took Mowgli to the Pack Council at the Council Rock. Akela, the great gray Lone Wolf who led the Pack, called for those who would speak for the cub. Baloo the sleepy brown bear rose and said, \"I speak for the man's cub. There is no harm in a man's cub. Let him run with the Pack.\" Bagheera the Black Panther added a fat bull as payment, and Mowgli was accepted into the Seeonee Wolf Pack.\n\nMowgli grew up with the wolf cubs, learning the Law of the Jungle from Baloo. He learned the Master Words that would protect him from all the jungle peoples, the Hunting People, the birds, and the Snake People. He learned to climb and swim and speak the languages of all creatures. But Baloo warned him never to have dealings with the Bandar-log, the Monkey People, who had no law and no memory.\n\nShere Khan never forgot his hatred for Mowgli. As Akela grew old and feeble, the tiger befriended the younger wolves, turning them against the man-cub. Bagheera warned Mowgli that the Pack would soon turn on him and that he must prepare for the day when Akela missed his kill.\n\nOne day, the Bandar-log kidnapped Mowgli and carried him through the treetops to the Cold Lairs, an ancient ruined city. They chattered and danced around him, boasting of their greatness. But Mowgli called on Rann the Kite for help, and Rann brought word to Baloo and Bagheera. The two friends came to rescue him, but the monkeys were too many. Baloo called on Kaa, the great Rock Python.\n\nKaa came and fought the monkeys with terrible power. His dance of hunger held them spellbound. \"Bandar-log,\" Kaa hissed, \"can ye stir foot or hand without my order? Speak!\" The monkeys were helpless in his fascination. Kaa broke the wall of the summerhouse and freed Mowgli. Bagheera gave Mowgli a beating for his foolishness, but all were grateful to Kaa.\n\nAs Akela grew older and weaker, the Pack turned against him and Mowgli. At the Council Rock, Shere Khan demanded the man-cub. Akela, who had missed his kill, was now called the Dead Wolf. He offered himself in Mowgli's place. But Mowgli stood before the Council with a fire-pot in his hands, the Red Flower that all jungle creatures fear.\n\n\"Ye have told me so often tonight that I am a man,\" Mowgli cried, \"that I feel your words are true. So I do not call ye my brothers any more, but dogs. I have brought here a little of the Red Flower which ye, dogs, fear.\" He flung the fire-pot and whirled a blazing branch above the cowering wolves. \"I go from you to my own people. But I promise that when I am a man among men I will not betray ye to men as ye have betrayed me. Now this debt to pay before I go!\"\n\nHe strode to Shere Khan and beat him with the branch. The tiger whimpered in fear. \"Singed jungle cat, go now! But remember when next I come to the Council Rock, it will be with Shere Khan's hide on my head.\" Then Mowgli went to his wolf mother and wept, for the jungle was shut to him. \"I will surely come,\" he said, \"and when I come it will be to lay out Shere Khan's hide upon the Council Rock.\"\n\nMowgli went down to the village and was taken in by Messua, a woman whose son had been carried off by a tiger. He learned the ways of men, though he found them strange. He herded buffaloes and grew strong. Gray Brother, one of the wolf cubs, brought him news that Shere Khan had returned and was hunting him.\n\nMowgli made a plan. He would drive the buffalo herd into the ravine where Shere Khan lay. With Akela's help, he divided the herd and drove the bulls down the ravine. The buffaloes trampled the tiger to death. Mowgli skinned Shere Khan and prepared to take the hide to the Council Rock.\n\nBut the villagers, led by the hunter Buldeo, called Mowgli a sorcerer and drove him out with stones. Messua wept, but Mowgli laughed and left the village with his wolves. He took Shere Khan's skin to the Council Rock and spread it out where Akela used to sit. \"Look well, O Wolves,\" he cried. \"Have I kept my word?\" The wolves bayed in answer.\n\nMowgli sang his song of triumph on the tiger's hide. Then he went to his mother's cave and said goodbye to Father Wolf and Mother Wolf. Bagheera came to him and said, \"Thou art a man, Little Brother. Thou must go to thy own people.\" But Mowgli knew he would never forget the jungle or his wolf brothers.\n\nSo Mowgli left the Council Rock, walking alone into the world of men, a boy who had been raised by wolves and who had killed the great tiger with his own cunning. He would always be part of the jungle, even when he became a man.\n\nThe book of the jungle holds more tales than one. A young mongoose named Rikki-Tikki-Tavi had been washed out of his burrow by a flood and rescued by a family in a bungalow in India. He was a curious, brave creature with a pink nose and a bottle-brush tail. He quickly made friends with the family, especially the boy Teddy.\n\nThe garden was full of danger. Nag, the great black cobra, and his wicked wife Nagaina lived there. They had already eaten one of Darzee the tailorbird's babies. Rikki-tikki fought Nag in the bathroom, and with the help of the big man's gun, Nag was killed. But Nagaina was still alive, and her eggs were hidden in the melon patch.\n\nRikki-tikki destroyed all but one of the eggs. Nagaina, desperate to save her last egg, threatened Teddy and his family. Rikki-tikki danced before her, keeping her from striking. \"Look at your eggs, Nagaina!\" he cried. \"The ants are eating all the others down by the melon bed.\" Nagaina turned in horror, and Teddy was pulled to safety. Rikki-tikki chased Nagaina into her hole and killed her.\n\nAll the birds and frogs sang with joy, for Nag and Nagaina would never terrorize the garden again. Rikki-tikki slept that night on Teddy's shoulder, proud and brave. He had earned his place as the guardian of the bungalow.\n\nThe White Seal, Kotick, was born with a pure white coat. He saw the seal hunters killing his people and swore to find a safe island where no men could come. He searched for years, asking all the sea creatures, until he found Sea Cow's tunnel leading to a hidden beach. He returned and fought his own kind to lead them to safety. The seals followed him to the new island, where men never came.\n\nLittle Toomai, the son of an elephant driver, was ten years old and longed to be a hunter. He went with Kala Nag, the great elephant, on a night journey and saw the elephants dance in a secret clearing. No man had ever seen this before. Petersen Sahib honored him and called him Toomai of the Elephants, and the elephants themselves saluted him with their trumpets.\n\nAnd so the jungle stories continue, tales of bravery and cunning, of the Law of the Jungle and the bonds between creatures. Mowgli, Rikki-Tikki, Kotick, and Toomai all found their place in the world, whether in the jungle, the sea, or among men.",
