    [
        'slug' => 'classic-gulliver',
        'gutenberg_id' => 829,
        'title' => 'Gulliver\'s Travels',
        'author' => 'Jonathan Swift',
        'license' => 'Public domain',
        'genres' => ['Adventure'],
        'quiz_difficulty' => 'medium',
        'cover' => 'covers/classic-gulliver.jpg',
        'synopsis' => 'Lemuel Gulliver\'s four extraordinary voyages take him from a land of tiny people to a kingdom of giants, from a floating island of absent-minded philosophers to a country where wise horses rule over brutish humans.',
        'text' => "My father had a small estate in Nottinghamshire. I was the third of five sons, sent to Cambridge at fourteen, then apprenticed to a London surgeon. After my master died and my practice failed, I went to sea. On May 4, 1699, we sailed from Bristol in the Antelope. A violent storm drove us toward Van Diemen's Land. On November 5, the ship struck a rock and split. I swam for my life, pushed forward by wind and tide, until I reached shore and fell into a deep sleep.\n\nWhen I awoke, I found my arms and legs fastened to the ground with slender ligatures. I felt something alive moving on my leg, and bending my eyes down, I perceived a human creature not six inches high, with a bow and arrow. Forty more followed. I roared so loud they ran back in fright. Struggling, I broke the strings on my left arm and loosened my hair. They shot arrows into my hand, pricking like needles. I lay still. A person of quality made a long speech, and I answered in the most submissive manner, putting my finger to my mouth to signify I wanted food.\n\nThey brought baskets of meat, shoulders and legs smaller than lark wings. I ate them two or three at a mouthful. They brought hogsheads of wine, which I drank off at a draught. A person of high rank from the emperor came forward, spoke for ten minutes, and pointed toward the capital city half a mile distant. I understood I must be carried as a prisoner. I gave tokens that they might do with me what they pleased.\n\nThis was Lilliput. The emperor and his court came out to meet me. I was lodged in an ancient temple, chained to the gate. The emperor, taller than his court by the breadth of my nail, surveyed me with admiration. His dress was plain, with a golden helmet adorned with jewels. I learned their language in about three weeks. The first words I learned were to express my desire for liberty. He answered that this must be a work of time.\n\nTwo officers searched my pockets and made an inventory. They found my watch, comb, snuff-box, pistols, scimitar, and many other strange items. The emperor ordered me to deliver them up. I drew my scimitar and waved it, dazzling their eyes. I discharged my pistols in the air, and hundreds fell down as if struck dead.\n\nMy gentleness gained me favor. I entertained the court with rope-dancing, where candidates for great offices jumped on a slender thread. I made a frame of sticks and stretched my handkerchief across it for twenty-four horses to exercise upon. The emperor was delighted. He told me, \"I have great confidence in your valor and strength, and I have commanded my principal secretary to lay before you the affairs of my kingdom.\"\n\nThe secretary, Reldresal, told me the kingdom labored under two mighty evils. One was a violent faction between the high-heels and low-heels. The other was the danger of invasion from Blefuscu, whose emperor had been at war with Lilliput for thirty-six moons over the proper end to break eggs. I offered to defend the emperor against all invaders.\n\nI waded into the channel with a cable and fifty hooks, fastened them to the prow of each of Blefuscu's fifty warships, and drew them after me. The Blefuscudians, seeing their whole fleet moving, \"set up such a scream of grief and despair as it is almost impossible to describe or conceive.\" The emperor created me a nardac, the highest title of honor. But when he wished me to bring the rest of their fleet and reduce Blefuscu to a province, I refused, saying \"I would never be an instrument of bringing a free and brave people into slavery.\" From this time began an intrigue against me.\n\nOne night the empress's apartment caught fire. I voided urine in such quantity that in three minutes the fire was wholly extinguished. But by the fundamental laws of the realm, it is capital to make water within the palace precincts. The empress conceived the greatest abhorrence of what I had done and vowed revenge.\n\nA friend warned me that articles of impeachment had been prepared against me, charging me with making water in the palace, with refusing to destroy Blefuscu, and with comforting their ambassadors. The council had determined to put out my eyes and starve me gradually. I fled to Blefuscu, where the emperor received me kindly. I found a boat overturned on the shore, fitted it up with the emperor's help, and set sail. On September 26, 1701, I was picked up by an English merchantman. The captain, Mr. John Biddel, was a very civil man. I showed him my cattle and the gold from Blefuscu, and he \"clearly convinced him of my veracity.\"\n\nI stayed only two months before my insatiable desire to see foreign countries sent me to sea again. On June 20, 1702, I sailed in the Adventure. A storm drove us far east. We came in view of a great island, and I went ashore with the long-boat to find water. I walked alone and saw the grass was twenty feet high. A reaper appeared as tall as a spire steeple. I hid in the corn until one of them discovered me and carried me to his master.\n\nThe farmer took me home. His wife screamed at the sight of me, but soon grew tender. His daughter, a girl of nine, became my nurse and taught me their language, giving me the name Grildrig. The farmer, at his friend's advice, showed me as a sight on market day. I performed tricks for crowds, nearly dead with weariness. The farmer grew insatiable and resolved to sell me.\n\nA gentleman-usher from court commanded my master to bring me to the queen. She was delighted and bought me for a thousand pieces of gold. I begged that Glumdalclitch, my little nurse, might remain with me. The king, a learned prince, thought I might be a piece of clockwork until he heard me speak. He questioned me about my country and was astonished.\n\nThe queen had a cabinet-maker build a box for me, sixteen feet square and twelve high, with windows and a door. I dined at the queen's table, and the king conversed with me about Europe. But he made very wise reflections, and after I had been too copious about my beloved country, he observed \"how contemptible a thing was human grandeur, which could be mimicked by such diminutive insects as I.\" He concluded the bulk of my natives to be \"the most pernicious race of little odious vermin that nature ever suffered to crawl upon the earth.\"\n\nI suffered many accidents. I was shaken by hail, carried off in a spaniel's mouth, attacked by wasps, and once a monkey seized me and carried me to a rooftop. The queen's dwarf tormented me continually until he was dismissed. I made a comb from the king's beard stumps and chairs from the queen's hair.\n\nAfter two years, I attended the king and queen on a progress to the south coast. Glumdalclitch was ill, and a page took me to the shore in my traveling-box. An eagle seized the ring of my box and carried it high, but was pursued by others and forced to drop me into the sea. I floated until a ship's crew found me. The captain, Thomas Wilcocks, took me aboard. I showed him my curiosities and told my story. He said, \"I hope, when we return to England, you will oblige the world by putting it on paper.\" I promised to take the matter into my thoughts. We arrived in England on June 3, 1706.\n\nI had not been home above ten days before Captain William Robinson invited me to be surgeon on his ship. We set out August 5, 1706. Off Tonquin, we were chased by two pirates. A Dutchman among them swore we should be tied back to back and thrown into the sea. I spoke Dutch and begged him for mercy, saying \"we were Christians and Protestants.\" This inflamed his rage. But the Japanese captain showed mercy and set me adrift in a canoe with provisions. I reached a rocky island and discovered a vast opaque body moving between me and the sun. It was a flying island inhabited by people who could raise or sink it as they pleased. They let down a chain with a seat, and I was drawn up to Laputa.\n\nThe Laputians were so taken up with intense speculations that they could not speak or listen without being roused by flappers who struck their mouths or ears. Their heads reclined, one eye turned inward, the other up to the zenith. They were great mathematicians and musicians. The king had no curiosity about my country, only about mathematics. The floating island moved by means of a great loadstone in its center, which could attract or repel the earth below, so the island could rise or descend over the king's dominions. If any town rebelled, the king could deprive them of sun and rain, or drop the island on their heads.\n\nI grew weary of these abstracted people and was recommended to a great lord in Balnibarbi. I visited the Academy of Lagado, where projectors labored on schemes such as extracting sunbeams from cucumbers, reducing excrement to its original food, calcining ice into gunpowder, building houses from the roof downward, and a machine that randomly arranged words to produce books of all arts and sciences. One professor told me, \"the most ignorant person, at a reasonable charge, and with a little bodily labor, might write books in philosophy, poetry, politics, laws, mathematics, and theology, without the least assistance from genius or study.\"\n\nI sailed to Glubbdubdrib, the island of sorcerers, where the governor could call up the dead. I saw Alexander the Great, Hannibal, Caesar, and Brutus. Homer and Aristotle appeared with their commentators, whom they despised. I saw the corruption of modern history exposed. The world had been misled by venal writers, and greatness and wealth were often won by crime.\n\nI sailed to Luggnagg, where I heard of the Struldbrugs, a race born with a red spot on the forehead who would never die. At first I thought this the greatest happiness, exclaiming, \"Happy nation, where every child hath at least a chance for being immortal!\" But I learned they grew melancholy, incapable of friendship, without memory or appetite, regarded as dead in law at eighty. They lived like foreigners in their own country, despised by all. My keen appetite for immortality was much abated.\n\nI reached Japan and sailed with a Dutch ship to Amsterdam, then returned to England on April 16, 1710, after five years and six months.\n\nI accepted command of the Adventurer. My crew conspired against me, bound me, and set me ashore on an unknown land. I saw deformed animals with human figures, eating roots and flesh. They were Yahoos, savage and detestable. Two horses approached and conversed with each other in a language I could almost understand. They examined me with wonder. I learned the word Yahoo and then Houyhnhnm, which meant horse, the perfection of nature.\n\nA gray horse, my master, led me to his house. I saw his family, horses sitting on their haunches, employed in domestic business. They kept Yahoos in kennels for labor. My master observed that I resembled a Yahoo but had some rudiments of reason. He taught me their language, and I learned that the Houyhnhnms were governed wholly by reason, without falsehood or evil. They knew nothing of lying. When I explained the concept, my master said, \"the use of speech was to make us understand one another. If any one said the thing which was not, these ends were defeated.\"\n\nI gave my master an account of England, telling him of our wars, laws, government, and the use of money. He asked what caused war between princes, and I told him of ambition, corruption, and differences in opinion over trifles. I described lawyers who proved white was black for a fee, physicians who killed as often as cured, and ministers who rose by bribery and betrayal. He listened with indignation and concluded that reason only served to multiply our vices.\n\nThe Houyhnhnms lived in perfect friendship and benevolence. They had no dispute, no crime, no disease. Their young were educated in temperance and hardiness. They married by reason, not love, and preserved their breed carefully. They had no letters, but their knowledge was traditional. In the grand assembly, a debate arose whether the Yahoos should be exterminated. My master proposed a plan that would tame them and over generations end their kind.\n\nI lived happily among them for three years, learning virtue. But my master told me the assembly had ordered I must depart. He said, \"the representatives had taken offense at his keeping a Yahoo (meaning myself) in his family.\" I fell into a swoon at his feet. He allowed me two months to make a canoe. With the help of the sorrel nag, I built a vessel of Yahoo skins and set sail on February 15, 1714-15. My master and his friends watched from shore, the sorrel nag crying out, \"Hnuy illa nyha, majah Yahoo,\" which means, \"Take care of thyself, gentle Yahoo.\"\n\nI reached New Holland but was wounded by an arrow from natives. A Portuguese ship found me, and the captain, Pedro de Mendez, treated me with great civility. He entreated me to give some account of myself, and desired to know what I would eat or drink, saying \"I should be used as well as himself.\" But I could not endure the sight of Yahoos and tried to throw myself into the sea. He restrained me and brought me to Lisbon, then to England. I arrived at my house on December 5, 1715, after five years away.\n\nMy wife and family received me with joy, but the sight filled me with hatred, disgust, and contempt. When my wife kissed me, I fell into a swoon. For years I could not endure them in my presence. I spent four hours each day conversing with my horses, who understood me. I live retired, applying the lessons of virtue I learned from the Houyhnhnms and teaching the Yahoos of my own family as far as I find them docible.\n\nI write not for fame but for the public good, to instruct mankind by the example of those admirable Houyhnhnms. I wish they would send some of their inhabitants to civilize Europe by teaching us the first principles of honor, justice, truth, temperance, and public spirit. I have now done with all visionary schemes forever, and return to enjoy my own speculations in my little garden, to lament the brutality to Houyhnhnms in my own country, and to treat their persons with respect for the sake of my noble master and his whole race.",
